<!--
CycloneDDS config for the cross-machine HiL run: controller.launch.py on the Jetson (10.10.10.1)
plus host.launch.py on the desktop (10.10.10.2), over a direct Ethernet cable. Point each end at
its own file with CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///ws/docker/cyclonedds-jetson.xml (or ...-host.xml). See
README "Cross-machine HiL over a direct link" for the static-IP setup this assumes.

Three things had to be pinned down; each one failed *silently*, with discovery simply never
completing and no error on either side.

1. FragmentSize. CycloneDDS's SPDP discovery payload is ~13.5KB (ROS2 nodes register a lot of
   parameter-service DDS endpoints), well over the ~1472-byte Ethernet MTU payload, so it gets
   IP-fragmented into ~10 packets. "netstat -s" on the Jetson showed most fragment reassemblies
   failing (only ~12% succeeded). Small single-packet tools ("ros2 multicast send/receive", plain
   "nc") never fragment and so never hit this, which is why they worked while real DDS discovery
   didn't. Capping the datagram below the path MTU makes CycloneDDS do its own reliable RTPS-level
   fragmentation (retried and reassembled within DDS) instead of trusting best-effort IP
   fragmentation. NIC offload (GRO/GSO/TSO) was a red herring, ruled out by disabling it on both
   ends with no change.

2. Interfaces. Both machines have several NICs (WiFi, docker0, tailscale0, and the direct link).
   Left to itself CycloneDDS picks one by its own heuristic and advertises that address as the
   participant's unicast locator. The desktop kept a leftover 169.254.x.x link-local address on
   the Ethernet NIC (auto-assigned during an earlier failed DHCP attempt) and advertised that one;
   the Jetson routes 169.254.0.0/16 out a down docker0, so every reply was black-holed. SPDP got
   through on multicast and SEDP (endpoint discovery, unicast) never did, giving a graph where the
   two sides see each other's participants but none of each other's topics. Pinning the interface
   by address is what makes the advertised locator deterministic.

3. Peers plus AllowMulticast. Multicast on this link needed a manual "ip route add 224.0.0.0/4 dev
   IFACE" on both ends, because the default route (WiFi) otherwise wins for 239.0.0.0/8. With
   exactly two known static peers there is no reason to depend on that: unicast SPDP to an
   explicit peer list takes multicast out of the picture entirely. ParticipantIndex is part of the
   same setting and not optional: it defaults to an ephemeral discovery port, which is unreachable
   for a peer that only has an address to aim at, so with multicast off and no index the peer list
   is useless and nothing discovers anything. "auto" gives each participant on a machine a
   deterministic port derived from its index, and MaxAutoParticipantIndex is how many of those a
   peer probes (one per node process, so 20 leaves room for the whole graph plus a stray shell).
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain>
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface address="10.10.10.1"/>
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
      <FragmentSize>1344B</FragmentSize>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>20</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="10.10.10.1"/>
        <Peer address="10.10.10.2"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
